00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:28.630
So we often hear that the language,
when we're trying to learn a language,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:34.965
consists of not just individual words,
but you know, chunks of, of words like

00:00:34.965 --> 00:00:39.825
phrases, uh, that there are certain
words that are typically always used

00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:44.445
with other words and these are called
collocations, like put to this...

00:00:44.475 --> 00:00:47.835
in the same location, you know,
that they combine with each other.

00:00:47.925 --> 00:00:54.495
Certainly, uh, there's a whole,
you know, industry, books and other

00:00:54.495 --> 00:01:00.675
publications on the subject of phrasal
verbs, uh, in English, you know, we get

00:01:00.675 --> 00:01:07.005
sick, get on the bus, get with it, get
going, all kind, uh, you know, take a

00:01:07.005 --> 00:01:13.395
hike, you know, there's all kinds of
phrases that go with certain verbs and

00:01:13.395 --> 00:01:15.765
these are now called phrasal verbs.

00:01:16.035 --> 00:01:19.015
So we have these terms:
collocations, phrasal verbs.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.380
This is not unique to English.

00:01:23.009 --> 00:01:27.060
Uh, although reading some of these
textbooks, you would think that it's

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:32.280
something unique to English that
you have, uh, expressions phrases,

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:37.590
where certain words will naturally
be combined with other words.

00:01:38.039 --> 00:01:39.390
Uh, but this happens elsewhere.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:41.700
I'm certainly seeing it in Persian.

00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.660
Uh, and I think it happens
in, in all languages.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.810
There's this thing about words,
it's, it's not only which words,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:53.280
words like to be friends with, you
know, colocate with, hang around

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:58.800
with, uh, there's also an issue of
order, like in terms of adjectives.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:01:59.490
It's amazing.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:07.500
If, if I'm gonna say there is
a new red expensive car, I'm

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:08.910
gonna say it in that order.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.070
And I can almost anticipate,
like, I'll just say it.

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:13.680
It's not an expensive new red car.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.490
It might be, but typically
there's a certain order.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:23.040
Uh, and, and, and so the native speaker
is gonna get it right most of the

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:25.050
time, but it's, it's not important.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:27.150
You can jumble the order.

00:02:27.155 --> 00:02:28.260
It's not so important.

00:02:28.950 --> 00:02:34.650
What is though interesting in all of
that is that these are habits in the

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.320
language, habits that we acquire.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:43.785
And so, you know, whenever I have tried
to learn set phrases, and they exist

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:48.234
in every language, even in even sort
of express sayings like raining cats

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:52.665
and dogs or the equivalent in other
languages, uh, I can never remember them

00:02:53.204 --> 00:02:54.975
because they don't come up that often.

00:02:55.035 --> 00:02:59.739
But for the native speaker, because the
native speaker has humongous quantity of

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:04.510
exposure to the language words they have
heard and read that they'll have come

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.410
across cats and dogs, raining cats and
dogs often enough that they will naturally

00:03:08.415 --> 00:03:10.510
use it when it's appropriate to use it.

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:14.920
The person who buys a book full
of English expressions, even

00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:18.910
if they can remember them, they
probably won't use them correctly.

00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.430
And so collocations, it's fine
to have that as a term, but

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:25.450
there are certain expressions...

00:03:25.454 --> 00:03:28.859
and I was just thinking about
this, for example, in English we

00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:33.600
say, as far as I know, far, as
far as I know, doesn't make sense.

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:34.019
Why?

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:38.250
As near as I know, as far as I know,
uh, you know, that expression could

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.910
have been as near as I know, because
we can say as near as I can tell,

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.779
as far as I can tell, as far as I
know, these are just expressions that

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.239
we kind of get used to, to using.

00:03:48.899 --> 00:03:54.000
And it's interesting, the equivalence in
other languages use a different imagery.

00:03:54.530 --> 00:04:07.364
Uh, you know, in Japanese ... like within
the limits of what I know ... or, um,

00:04:08.224 --> 00:04:14.934
uh, again with the sub subjective or,
um, somewhat similar in Russian ... uh,

00:04:16.164 --> 00:04:20.954
what else have we got, uh, ... you
know, in Chinese, again, it's different.

00:04:22.185 --> 00:04:28.105
The problem is that if any speakers
of those languages try to translate

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:31.455
from those expressions into English,
the resulting expression will

00:04:31.455 --> 00:04:34.425
sound funny: as much as I know.

00:04:34.485 --> 00:04:37.005
No, no we say as far as I know,
we don't say as much as I know,

00:04:37.155 --> 00:04:38.775
or as many as I know from Russian.

00:04:38.865 --> 00:04:42.555
As I know from Chinese or within
the limits that I know from

00:04:42.555 --> 00:04:43.935
Japanese, we just don't say that.

00:04:44.849 --> 00:04:45.869
How do you get...

00:04:45.869 --> 00:04:50.640
and, and the number of these phrases
and collocations and phrasal verbs,

00:04:50.640 --> 00:04:54.750
they're, they're almost unlimited
so that, you know, first of all, the

00:04:54.755 --> 00:04:58.559
idea that you could buy a dictionary
of these and somehow learn them and

00:04:58.559 --> 00:05:03.284
sprinkle your language with these i,
I think is a little bit unrealistic.

00:05:03.405 --> 00:05:08.534
If you already know them, if you have
already come across them in different, um,

00:05:08.594 --> 00:05:15.344
contexts and you then find a book which is
full of these, and especially if it's, you

00:05:15.344 --> 00:05:19.630
know, in the same language, so that you're
kind of exposing yourself to the language

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:22.930
and reading up on useful expressions
and staying in the language when you

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.090
are already at a fairly advanced level.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.670
You know, I can see that as
a, as being a useful exercise.

00:05:27.810 --> 00:05:31.960
However, I think the, the only way,
and this is a familiar theme with

00:05:31.965 --> 00:05:36.719
me, the only way that you can become
confident in using these is if you have

00:05:36.719 --> 00:05:38.669
had enough exposure to the language.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.190
This, by the way, also applies to slang.

00:05:41.310 --> 00:05:47.250
It applies to swear words, which to
my mind are taboo, uh, until you are

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:51.810
comfortable enough in the language that
you can use them naturally and correctly.

00:05:52.169 --> 00:05:56.460
It, it applies to, for example, in
Chinese, they have ...which are these

00:05:56.460 --> 00:06:03.630
sort of very literate sounding, uh,
four character expressions that usually

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:07.080
have a reference to something in
Chinese history or Chinese literature.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:12.750
And if you use them, you sound very
erudite, but I've never been able

00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:16.560
to remember enough of them so that I
could sprinkle them through my Chinese

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.570
conversation and, and sound like, uh, you
know, and impress the Chinese speaker.

00:06:22.080 --> 00:06:30.120
Um, so I, I think this whole idea
that you can sort of zero in on

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.115
phrasal verbs, collocations and
thereby enrich your language...

00:06:35.505 --> 00:06:39.495
I think this might be possible when
you are already fairly advanced

00:06:39.500 --> 00:06:43.455
in the language and you're doing
it out of interest and for fun and

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.664
with a realization that you won't
remember much, you won't remember

00:06:46.664 --> 00:06:50.174
many of these expressions, but
it's fun, kind of reviewing them.

00:06:50.865 --> 00:06:54.195
But at an earlier stage in the
language, if you think you can sort

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:00.930
uh, leverage yourself up, ladder
yourself up by focusing in on

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.560
phrasal verbs and, um, collocations.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:08.160
I think you may be disappointed
and I think that you're still

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:13.590
better off to continue to massively
expose yourself to the language.

00:07:14.010 --> 00:07:19.680
And you'd be surprised at how often
these expressions that are almost

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:23.115
limitless show up in all the things
that you're reading and listening to.

00:07:23.255 --> 00:07:27.405
And so you'll, your brain will naturally
get used to the fact that certain words

00:07:27.525 --> 00:07:29.205
are used with certain other words.

00:07:29.685 --> 00:07:34.185
So that's my take on
collocations and phrasal verbs.

00:07:34.575 --> 00:07:36.285
Uh, I hope you found that useful.

00:07:36.915 --> 00:07:37.455
Thank you.

00:07:38.645 --> 00:07:39.015
Bye for now.

